            "pageCount": 1,
            "processed": true,
            "summary": "The document details a case involving a 45-year-old man who recently commenced treatment with Glucoreg for type 2 diabetes. Following his morning dose, he experienced mild nausea and vomiting about one hour later, which recurred over several days. These symptoms, although bothersome, did not necessitate medical attention. To address the issue, the timing of the dose was modified to be taken with food. After this adjustment, the patient reported a significant improvement in his symptoms.\n\nThis document is relevant to a patient-safety review because it describes an adverse reaction (nausea and vomiting) associated with a medication. It outlines the patient's experience with these symptoms and how a change in the administration of the drug helped alleviate the reaction. The case provides insights into potential side effects of Glucoreg and emphasizes the importance of proper dosing instructions, particularly in relation to food. Monitoring such reactions contributes to overall pharmacovigilance efforts and ensures patient safety."
        }
